content.text:
The view-flow machinery is verified and saved. I also identified a key simplification that makes the remaining resolution theorem more tractable than general precise typing:

**Observation:** under `HInv`, every object field type comes from `record_dec` and is therefore either `inert_typ` (an arrow or a record-bodied μ) or a singleton. Consequently:
- a `μ`/record/singleton field type cannot subtype an arrow unless it already is the needed shape;
- for an object flow output `U`, `flow_mu_shape`/`flow_record_shape` restrict `U` to `μT0` or a syntactic record type, so record-target refinement only needs record/and constructor reasoning, not arbitrary type-selection chains;
- arrow targets resolve to tight λ values, record targets to object definitions, and singleton targets to path aliases.

This supports a `TR` result relation for ordinary path resolution:
- `TR_lam`: tight λ with `S0 <: T`;
- `TR_obj`: ν object with `record_type T0`, precise flow `p : μT0 ⪼ U`, and `U <: T`;
- `TR_alias`: path alias at a singleton;
with field cases using `defs_field_lookup`/`flow_mu_record_has` (heap view) or `flow_open_record_has` (self view) and `ty_def_all`/`ty_def_new`/`ty_def_path` inversion.

**Verified state:** the full chain through `selfflow.v` and `viewflow.v` compiles with no assumptions. `/workspace/Task.v` remains the supplied skeleton; `safety` is still unproved. All work is persistently backed up.

